1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,175
Debug a transactional gate release and
print the runtime class of the injected
collaborator. You expected
GateReleaseServiceImpl. You see something
like $Proxy147 or
GateReleaseServiceImpl$$SpringCGLIB$$0.

2
00:00:16,295 --> 00:00:24,792
The method still opens the boom.
Transactions still commit. So what object
did Spring actually inject?

3
00:00:24,972 --> 00:00:32,603
A proxy — a runtime stand-in that looks
like your bean to callers, holds the real
target, and decides what happens on each
invocation.

4
00:00:32,724 --> 00:00:47,172
Dynamic proxies are synthesized at
runtime, not handwritten as
GateReleaseServiceProxy for every type.
Without that stand-in, AOP has nowhere to
stand. A client calls
gateService.release(cargoId).

5
00:00:47,292 --> 00:01:00,036
If gateService is the raw target,
@Transactional and your @Around never run.
If it is a proxy, the call enters the
invocation handler, the interceptor chain
runs, and only then does proceed reach the
target.

6
00:01:00,155 --> 00:01:15,683
Look at this java for a moment. Look at
this java for a moment.
Proxy.newProxyInstance needs a class
loader, the interfaces to implement, and
an InvocationHandler. Every interface
method becomes invoke on the handler.

7
00:01:15,804 --> 00:01:20,867
Spring’s AOP runtime is that idea with an
advisor chain, pointcut matching,

8
00:01:20,987 --> 00:01:29,388
and ReflectiveMethodInvocation calling
proceed through interceptors — including
transaction and security interceptors you
did not author by hand.

9
00:01:29,507 --> 00:01:40,283
Two families matter. If the bean exposes
at least one interface, Spring may create
a JDK dynamic proxy that implements those
interfaces and delegates to the target.

10
00:01:40,403 --> 00:01:52,787
If the bean is a concrete class with no
interface — or you force class-based
proxies — Spring uses CGLIB to subclass
the concrete type and override methods.
Same idea for callers: talk to a wrapper.

11
00:01:52,908 --> 00:02:09,731
Different mechanics under the floor. Why
“dynamic”? The proxy class is generated as
the context refreshes. You do not maintain
GateServiceProxy.java. Auto-proxy creators
wrap eligible beans when advisors match.

12
00:02:09,852 --> 00:02:23,243
Operational consequences follow.
Collaborators receive the proxy, which is
what you want for advice to fire. Casting
to the concrete impl can fail when the
runtime chose a JDK interface proxy.

13
00:02:23,364 --> 00:02:36,156
Self-calls inside the target
(this.release(...)) never enter the proxy,
so annotated advice on that path is
skipped. @Async, @Transactional, and
custom @Aspect advice all share that
requirement.

14
00:02:36,276 --> 00:02:47,915
People sometimes imagine AOP as a compiler
plugin rewriting every .class. Full
AspectJ can weave that way. Spring AOP’s
everyday path is proxy-based.

15
00:02:48,036 --> 00:03:00,515
Confusing the two leads to surprise when
private methods or field access are not
advised. So the injected object for a
transactional gate service is a proxy:
interceptor logic first, then the target.

16
00:03:00,635 --> 00:03:10,115
The next split is which technology Spring
picks — and what breaks when you assume
the wrong one. Start with the interface-
based path: JDK Proxy.
